  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/6d5146afb9508532145180a4f632c0b5c4495487b0a9a89cfd4f71e134b95f50.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{\u2032}(-1.5).$",
  "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{\u2032}(-1.5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-1.5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-2,1].$ Notice that $x=-1.5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/2216d3014a8c74102d5e298c300092a575798de0b66d89914716898bc16ac655.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x} & =\\frac{0-2}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(-1.5)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$",
